  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 142 PM MDT Sun Aug 16 2026\n\nAfter a briefly warmer and drier day on Monday, a series of \nshortwave troughs aloft will move over the top of the ridge and \nflatten it a bit, lowering heights aloft and 700-mb temperatures for \nTuesday and Wednesday. While the flow direction aloft will remain \nwesterly, we should see a fetch of better recycled monsoon moisture \nmoving into the area. Precipitable water will nudge up slightly for \nTuesday, back to between 0.75\" and 1.1\" in Wyoming and 1.0\" to 1.25\" \nin Nebraska. The boundary layer (especially on the Wyoming side) \nwill probably remain fairly dry Tuesday as remnants of Monday's \ndrier airmass remain in place, but fairly plentiful moisture will \npush in aloft. Forcing from the passage of the upper level shortwave \ntrough will support scattered showers and thunderstorms developing \nin the afternoon and evening hours, likely greater coverage than \nMonday's activity. Rainfall will not have trouble reaching the \nground despite the drier boundary layer due to the high precipitable \nwater. Wednesday will bring a fairly similar setup, but probably \nwith better surface moisture due to models picking up on slightly \nmore organized southerly flow across the High Plains. While it is \nstill early, parameters look somewhat favorable for strong to severe \nthunderstorms both Tuesday and Wednesday, despite lower overall \nthunderstorm coverage compared to the monsoon surge our area has \nbeen under for the last several days. The ECMWF ensemble Extreme \nForecast Index for combined CAPE-Shear is around 0.7 to 0.85 for \nmuch of the High Plains both Tuesday and Wednesday, indicating that \na large proportion of ECMWF ensemble members have combined CAPE-\nshear values that are climatologically unusual to extreme. We will \nneed to continue to watch this period in the coming days. \n\nA more defined upper level shortwave trough will pass through the \narea Wednesday evening. Behind this feature, the upper level ridge \nover the southwest will begin to re-amplify to our west, initiating \na drier, northwest flow pattern for the remainder of the week. \nPrecipitable water won't be terrible during this weather pattern, so \nwe should still see at least a few isolated PM showers and storms \nThursday and Friday, but expect those two days to be considerably \ndrier. Temperatures will also begin to climb once again. 700-mb \ntemperatures climb back to near the climatological 90th percentile \nby Friday into Saturday, peaking around +16 to +18C across the area. \nExpect high temperatures to climb above seasonal averages Thursday, \nand then closer to 5 to 10 degrees above average Friday and \nSaturday. Since we'll be somewhat on the periphery of this heat \nwave, current forecasts do not challenge daily record highs, but \nthis could still change in the coming days. Over the course of \nThursday through Saturday, the upper level ridge should start to \nvery slowly meander back to the east. Once this moves east of the \nFour Corners, the monsoon should surge back to the north, and we may \nsee precipitation chances increasing again by Saturday.",
